  "transcription": "Curved waveguide having a ...\n\n\\[ \\frac{1}{r} \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial r} \\left( r \\frac{\\partial \\Pi^{(n)}}{\\partial r} \\right) + \\frac{1}{r^2} \\frac{\\partial^2 \\Pi^{(n)}}{\\partial \\varphi^2} + \\frac{\\partial^2 \\Pi^{(n)}}{\\partial z^2} + k^2 \\Pi^{(n)} = 0. \\quad (1) \\]\n\nIts solution is found in the form of a linear combination of the Bessel\nand Neumann functions: \\[ W_n = J_{\\delta_n}(x) + \\eta_n N_{\\delta_n}(x) \\quad (5). \\]\n\n\\[ \\eta_n \\] is a constant which has different values for different n. \\[ \\delta_n \\] is the\nsolution of the transcendental equation:\n\\[ J_{\\delta_n}(y) N_{\\delta_n}(\\varepsilon y) - J_{\\delta_n}(\\varepsilon y) N_{\\delta_n}(y) = 0 \\quad (6). \\]\n\n\\[ \\delta_n^2 = - \\beta_n^2 \\] and \\[ \\beta \\] denotes the propagation factor. \\[ \\varepsilon = \\sqrt{\\mu \\varepsilon} / q \\]. It is pointed\nout that the numerical values \\[ W_1 \\], i.e., the field distribution of the\nwave propagating in the guide cross section is very hard to calculate,\nsince there is no table for Bessel functions of arbitrary fractions.\n\nCard 2/9",
